The io_uring security dilemma. And a serious non-blocking alternative

60% of Google's kernel exploit bounties targeted io_uring with attacks like ROP. Docker disabled it by default. Same decision for Android and ChromeOS. Google's own servers disabled it.

Yet high-performance databases are building on io_uring as their fast I/O path. TigerBeetle, parts of ScyllaDB, and many others have deep io_uring dependencies.

What happens when your security team says "no io_uring"? You fall back to synchronous I/O. Performance tanks.

But there is a truly non-blocking, battle-tested alternative.

FreeBSD AIO + kqueue provides the same completion-based async I/O model. It is truly kernel-level non-blocking (not a thread-pool simulation) and comes with 25 years of security hardening instead of 5 years of CVEs.

• Netflix serves 30% of US internet traffic on FreeBSD.
• WhatsApp handled 2M concurrent connections per server on FreeBSD.
• ZFS 2.3 added O_DIRECT support, closing the last performance gap.

I've been exploring this architecture for a TigerBeetle database port. The deep-dive revealed something surprising: the technical foundation is solid, validated by PostgreSQL's core team, and production-proven at massive scale.

Full technical analysis: https://lnkd.in/eXbs2WA5

FreeBSD AIO + kqueue + ZFS provides a security-conscious alternative to Linux io_uring.

The syscall overhead is a performance tradeoff, but for security-sensitive deployments, this is the correct path.

